\end{document}


This variance is proportional to $\sigma_u^2 = u^T V u / n$ where
$V=\hat\Sigma^{-1}\hat\Sigma_w \hat\Sigma^{-1}$, which is $n$ times the
covariance matrix of $\hat\beta - \beta$, is a 'sandwich' of the
weighted covariate covariance $\hat\Sigma_w$ between two slices of the
unweighted covariance matrix $\hat \Sigma$'s inverse. As a result,
$u^T(\hat \beta - \beta) / \hat \sigma_u$ is normal with mean zero and
variance one---it's standard normal. We call this a *Z-statistic*. Note
that because the standard normal is symmetric around zero in the sense
that $Z$ and $-Z$ have the same distribution, it's also true that
$u^T(\beta - \hat \beta) / \hat\sigma_u$ is standard normal.

We know that a standard normal random variable $Z$ is in the interval
$[-1,1]$ with probability $\approx .68$, in the interval $[-2,2]$ with
probability $\approx .95$, and in the interval $[-3,3]$ with probability
$\approx .997$. And it follows via arithmetic that if $(Z-\mu)/\sigma$
is standard normal, then $Z$ is in the interval
$[\mu-\sigma,\mu+\sigma]$ with probability $\approx .68$, in the
interval $[\mu-2\sigma,\mu+2\sigma]$ with probability $\approx .95$, and
in the interval $[\mu-3\sigma, \mu+3\sigma]$ with probability
$\approx .997$. Thus, $u^T\beta$ is in the interval
$[u^T\hat\beta - \hat\sigma_u, u^T \hat\beta + \hat \sigma_u]$ with
probability $\approx .68$. This is an $\approx 68\%$ confidence interval
for $u^T \beta$.

Let's make this a bit more concrete. Suppose we want a confidence
interval for the conditional mean outcome $\mu(x)$ at some point $x$.
Given our assumptions, that's just $u^T \beta$ for $u=\phi(x)$, so we
have a $Z$-statistic
$$ Z=\frac{\hat\mu(x) - \mu(x)}{\hat\sigma} \quad \text{ where } \quad 
\hat\sigma_u^2 = \frac{1}{n} \phi(x)^T \hat \Sigma^{-1} \hat \Sigma_w \hat\Sigma^{-1},\quad \hat \Sigma=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \phi(X_i) \phi(X_i)^T,\quad \hat\Sigma_w =\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma(X_i)^2 \phi(X_i) \phi(X_i)^T. $$
Rearranging this, we get a 68% confidence interval
$[\hat \mu(x) - \hat \sigma_u, \hat \mu(x) + \hat \sigma_u]$.

::: exercise
Below, I've written code for generating some data. Run it 1000 times to
generate 1000 samples of size 100. For each sample, fit the linear model
$\{m(x)=\beta_0 + \beta_1 x : \beta_0, \beta_1 \in \mathbb{R} \}$,
compute the $Z$-statistic $Z=\{\hat\mu(1) - \mu(1)\} / \hat\sigma_u$ and
the associated 68% confidence interval for $\mu(1)$. Then (i) plot a
histogram of your 1000 $Z$-statistics and (ii) report report the number
of times $\mu(1)$ falls in your confidence interval.

We call the fraction of times this happens the confidence interval's
*coverage*, and we say our interval has *nominal coverage* if it has the
coverage we claim it has, i.e. if a 68% interval has 68% coverage.
:::

Here's the code that generates the data.

```{r}
beta = c(1,2)
phi = function(x) { rbind(1,x) }
mu = function(x) { t(phi(x)) %*% beta }
sigma = function(x) { 1/(1+abs(x)) }
n = 100

X = rnorm(n)
Z = rnorm(n)
Y   = mu(X) + sigma(X)*Z
```

I'm going to include a solution to this one. So far, we've done pretty
much everything using built-in functions and packages. We've talked
about matrices and vectors and all that, but we haven't actually used
them to implement anything. That'll have to change a little now. For
whatever reason, unless you're interested in estimating some very
specific and typically uninteresting things, e.g. a single coefficient
$\beta_j$, you tend to have to do a fair amount yourself.

::: solution
Our histogram looks standard normal and our coverage is what we say it is: 68%.
```{r}
# Describe Distribution of the Data
set.seed(1)

beta = c(1,2)
phi = function(x) { rbind(1,x) }
mu = function(x) { t(phi(x)) %*% beta }
sigma = function(x) { 1/(1+abs(x)) }
n = 100

Z.stats = array(dim=10000)
covers  = array(dim=10000)
for(rr in 1:1000) {
  # Sample Data
  X = rnorm(n)
  Z = rnorm(n)
  Y   = mu(X) + sigma(X)*Z

  ggplot() + geom_point(aes(x=X,y=Y))
  # Fit model
  model = lm(Y~1+X)
  mu.hat.1 = predict(model, newdata=data.frame(X=1))
  
  # Calculate V and sigma.hat.u
  Sigma.hat = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { Sigma.hat = Sigma.hat + phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n }
  Sigma.hat.inv = solve(Sigma.hat)
  Sigma.hat.w = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { 
    Sigma.hat.w = Sigma.hat.w + sigma(X[i])^2 * phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n 
  }
  V = Sigma.hat.inv %*% Sigma.hat.w %*% Sigma.hat.inv
  sigma.hat.u = sqrt( t(phi(1)) %*% V %*% phi(1) / n )
  
  # Calculate Z stat and Interval
  Z = (mu.hat.1 - mu(1) )/sigma.hat.u  # in [-1, 1] wp .68
  a = mu.hat.1 - sigma.hat.u             
  b = mu.hat.1 + sigma.hat.u
  
  ## Record Results
  Z.stats[rr]=Z
  covers[rr]  = a <= mu(1) & mu(1) <= b
}

ggplot() + geom_histogram(aes(x=Z.stats), bins=60)
mean(covers)
```
:::

In your calculation of the variance $u^T V u$, you've probably used the
function $\sigma(x)=1/(1+\lvert x \rvert)$ written in the code
above---the standard deviation of $\varepsilon_i$. We don't know that in
real data, so let's try using something we can use. Instead of
$\sigma(X_i)^2 = E \varepsilon_i^2$, let's use the squared residual
$\hat\varepsilon_i^2 = \{ Y_i - \phi(X_i)^T \hat \beta \}^2$.

::: exercise
Modify your code from the last exercise to use this new
implementable-in-real-data approach to variance estimation. Do you see
much of a difference in coverage? Repeat both exercises in samples of
size $n=10$, $n=20$, and $n=1000$ and see if that changes.
:::

::: solution
In larger sample sizes, like 100 or 1000, coverage is almost exactly the same as before.
In smaller sample sizes, like 10 or 20, it's a bit lower. If you look at the histogram,
you can see that in smaller samples the peak in the middle is higher and the tails at either the end are higher (we actually say heavier) too. At sample size 10 we see Z-statistics from -5 to 5, whereas at sample sizes
100+ they more or less random from -3 to 3. Because the distribution in small samples has this not-quite-normal shape, acting like it's normal leads to invalid claims about coverage.

Let me explain what's going on here. In large sample sizes, our estimate of the variance is more or less non-random. That's essentially about the law of large numbers: in large samples, averages converge to their expectations. So when we divide by our estimated standard deviation, we're effectively dividing by the 
actual standard deviation. In small samples, averages vary above and below their expectations. That means sometimes we're dividing by something smaller than the actual standard deviation of our estimator:
that's why we see higher, or heavier, tails on our distribution. And sometimes we're dividing by something bigger than the actual standard deviation of our estimator: that's why we see a higher peak in the middle.
Flipping 3 or 4 coins, fitting a horizontal line (i.e. looking at the sample mean), and calculating Z-statistics, can be a good way to build intuition for this phenomenon. We're not going to
talk about this again or see this on the exam, but if you hear about the `t distribution`, it may be helpful to know that it's something like this: it's the distribution of an average of standard normal random variables divided by an estimate of its standard deviation, and has this higher peak in the center and heavier tails.

```{r}
set.seed(1)

# Describe Distribution of the Data
beta = c(1,2)
phi = function(x) { rbind(1,x) }
mu = function(x) { t(phi(x)) %*% beta }
sigma = function(x) { 1/(1+abs(x)) }
n = 100

Z.stats = array(dim=1000)
covers  = array(dim=1000)
for(rr in 1:1000) {
  # Sample Data
  X = rnorm(n)
  Z = rnorm(n)
  Y   = mu(X) + sigma(X)*Z

  # Fit model
  model = lm(Y~1+X)
  mu.hat.1 = predict(model, newdata=data.frame(X=1))
  epsilon.hat = Y - predict(model, newdata=data.frame(X=X))                                 # This line was added
  
  # Calculate V and sigma.hat.u
  Sigma.hat = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { Sigma.hat = Sigma.hat + phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n }
  Sigma.hat.inv = solve(Sigma.hat)
  Sigma.hat.w = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { 
    # This line changed
    Sigma.hat.w = Sigma.hat.w + epsilon.hat[i]^2 * phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n 
  } 
  V = Sigma.hat.inv %*% Sigma.hat.w %*% Sigma.hat.inv
  sigma.hat.u = sqrt( t(phi(1)) %*% V %*% phi(1) / n )
  
  # Calculate Z stat and Interval
  Z = (mu.hat.1 - mu(1) ) / sigma.hat.u
  a = mu.hat.1 - sigma.hat.u
  b = mu.hat.1 + sigma.hat.u
  
  ## Record Results
  Z.stats[rr] = Z
  covers[rr]  = a <= mu(1) & mu(1) <= b
}

ggplot() + geom_histogram(aes(x=Z.stats), bins=60)
mean(covers)
```
:::

::: exercise
Repeat the last two exercises using $\mu(x)=\sin(x)$ in place of
$\mu(x)=1+2x$ when generating the data. Does everything still work? Why
or why not? You can do this one at sample size $n=100$. There's no need
to repeat it at others.

Here's a hint. Take a look at your histogram. And try looking at the
histogram of a different Z-statistic,
$\{\hat\mu(1) - \tilde\mu(1)\} / \hat\sigma_u$, and how often
$\tilde\mu(1)$ is in your confidence interval. In lecture a few weeks
ago, we showed that $\tilde\mu(x)=x/\sqrt{e}$.
:::

::: solution
What we see in the Z-statistic histograms is that if we look at the one we've been
using, where we subtract $\mu(1)$, we're way off-center. That's the purple histogram,
which is centered at -2.5 or so. As a result, our confidence intervals, which are
based on the assumption that these Z-statistics are standard normal, don't work very well
if we think of them as intervals meant to contain $\mu(1)$. Coverage is bad.

The green histogram, which shows Z-statistics in which we've subtracted $\tilde\mu(1)$, 
is centered at zero and, as before more or less standard normal at sample size 100. As a result,
if we think of our confidence intervals as intervals meant to contain $\tilde\mu(1)$, they do work.
Coverage is good. If we look at smaller sample sizes, e.g. n=10 and n=20, we see exactly the same phenomenon
we saw in the previous example. Coverage gets worse because the distribution of Z-statistics gets
to have a higher peak and heavier tails. But that has nothing to do with the fact we're using a $\sin$
curve that our model can't fit. We're seeing coverage for $\tilde\mu(1)$ like we saw for $\mu(1)$
in the last exercise.

In short, our confidence intervals work for $\tilde\mu(1)$. It doesn't matter whether our model 
is right in the sense that it contains the conditional mean $\mu$, like in the last exercise, or 
it's way off, like in this one. What changes as a result of our choice of model is the degree to 
which $\tilde\mu(1)$ is a good approximation to $\mu(1)$, and therefore the degree to which our 
confidence intervals work for $\mu(1)$.

```{r}
set.seed(1)

# Describe Distribution of the Data
beta = c(1,2)
phi = function(x) { rbind(1,x) }
mu = sin                                       # This line changed
mu.tilde = function(x) { x/sqrt(exp(1)) }      # This line was added
sigma = function(x) { 1/(1+abs(x)) }
n=100

Z.stats = array(dim=1000)
covers  = array(dim=1000)
Z.stats.mu.tilde = array(dim=1000)             # This line was added
covers.mu.tilde = array(dim=1000)              # This line was added
for(rr in 1:1000) {
  # Sample Data
  X = rnorm(n)
  Z = rnorm(n)
  Y   = mu(X) + sigma(X)*Z

  # Fit model
  model = lm(Y~1+X)
  mu.hat.1 = predict(model, newdata=data.frame(X=1))
  epsilon.hat = Y - predict(model, newdata=data.frame(X=X))                                    
  
  # Calculate V and sigma.hat.u
  Sigma.hat = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { Sigma.hat = Sigma.hat + phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n }
  Sigma.hat.inv = solve(Sigma.hat)
  Sigma.hat.w = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { 
    Sigma.hat.w = Sigma.hat.w + epsilon.hat[i]^2 * phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n 
  } 
  V = Sigma.hat.inv %*% Sigma.hat.w %*% Sigma.hat.inv
  sigma.hat.u = sqrt( t(phi(1)) %*% V %*% phi(1) / n )
  
  # Calculate Z stat and Interval
  Z = (mu.hat.1 - mu(1) ) / sigma.hat.u
  a = mu.hat.1 - sigma.hat.u
  b = mu.hat.1 + sigma.hat.u
  
  ## Record Results
  Z.stats[rr] = Z
  covers[rr]  = a <= mu(1) & mu(1) <= b
  
  Z.stats.mu.tilde[rr] = (mu.hat.1 - mu.tilde(1)) /sigma.hat.u  # added
  covers.mu.tilde[rr] = a <= mu.tilde(1) & mu.tilde(1) <= b     # added 
}
ggplot() + geom_histogram(aes(x=Z.stats), bins=60, fill='purple', alpha=.2) +
           geom_histogram(aes(x=Z.stats.mu.tilde), bins=60, fill='green', alpha=.2) 
mean(covers)
mean(covers.mu.tilde)
```
:::

## Least Squares in General

Now let's think about what happens without weird assumptions.

The coefficients $\hat\beta$ solving our least squares problem don't
change. They're $$ \hat \beta = \hat\Sigma^{-1}\phi^Y \quad 
\text{ for } \quad \hat \Sigma = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \phi(X_i)\phi(X_i)^T \quad 
\text{ and } \quad \phi^Y = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \phi(X_i)Y_i. $$We
can still break this down into two parts, the first being a signal part
and the second a sort of noise part. To do this, we use the
decomposition $Y_i = \phi(X_i)^T \tilde\beta + \tilde \varepsilon_i$,
where
$$ \tilde \beta = \text{arg}\min_{\beta} E \{ \phi(X_i)^T \beta  - Y_i \}^2 \quad \text{ and } \quad \tilde\varepsilon_i = Y_i - \phi(X_i)^T \tilde \beta. $$
And we've shown previously in class that $\tilde\varepsilon_i$ has a
sort of population version of the properties we look for in our residual
plots.
$$ E m(X_i) \tilde\varepsilon_i = 0 \quad \text{ for all curves } \quad m(x)=\phi(X_i)^T \beta \quad \text{ in our model, or equivalently, }
   E \phi(X_i) \tilde\varepsilon_i = 0. $$ Let's see what this does for
us. Breaking down $\hat \beta$, we get $$ 
\hat \beta = \hat \Sigma^{-1} \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \phi(X_i) \underbrace{\phi(X_i)^T \tilde\beta}_{\tilde\mu(X_i)} + \hat\Sigma^{-1}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \phi(X_i)\tilde\varepsilon_i.
$$ The first term here is just
$\hat\Sigma^{-1}\hat\Sigma\tilde\beta=\tilde\beta$, so subtracting it to
the left side gives us a formula for our error in estimating these
coefficients.
$$ \hat \beta - \tilde\beta = \hat\Sigma^{-1}\underbrace{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \phi(X_i)\tilde\varepsilon_i}_{\phi^{\tilde\varepsilon}}. $$
In the average $\phi^{\tilde\varepsilon}$ here, the terms
$\phi(X_i)\tilde\varepsilon_i$ are independent and they have mean zero
because of this special property of $\tilde\varepsilon_i$. As a result,
by the central limit theorem, we should expect it to behave roughly like
an analogous sum in which $\tilde\varepsilon$ were normal. That's what
makes all this work. One thing we don't have now, however, is the
ability to think of $X_i$ as fixed: our central limit theorem depends on
an expectation in which we average over $X_i$. But we can still proceed
essentially as before.

To make sense of this, let's think about what happens if we replace
$\hat\Sigma$ and $\phi^{\tilde\varepsilon}$ by their limits. That is,
we'll substitute for $\hat \Sigma$ the deterministic matrix
$\Sigma = E \phi(X_i)\phi(X_i)^T$ that the law of large numbers implies
it converges to and substitute for the sum the normal random vector with
covariance matrix $(1/n)\Sigma_w$ for
$\Sigma_w = E \{ \phi(X_i)\tilde\varepsilon_i\}^T \{ \phi(X_i)\tilde\varepsilon_i\}$
that the central limit theorem implies that it converges to. Their
product is a normal random vector with covariance matrix
$(1/n)\Sigma^{-1} \Sigma_w \Sigma^{-1}$, i.e. if we call their product
$\Delta$, then $u^T \Delta$ is normal with variance $u^T V u$ for
$V=\Sigma^{-1} \Sigma_w \Sigma^{-1}$. This means that if we can get away
with acting as if $\hat \Sigma$ and its limit and
$\phi^{\tilde\varepsilon}$ and its limit as equivalent, then
$u^T (\hat\beta - \tilde \beta)$ has exactly the same distribution that
$u^T (\hat\beta -\beta)$ had using our simple assumptions. It's not
going to be exactly the same, as we are making approximations, but our
approximations and therefore this approximate equivalence get
increasingly good in large samples. We'll dig into how this works with
pen and paper a little in the next homework. For now, let's see what
happens empirically.

Before that, let's summarize why this all works.

1\. The fact that our model is wrong, so $\tilde \mu \neq \mu$, doesn't
change much because $\phi(X_i)^T \tilde\varepsilon_i$ still has mean
zero. It just means that the distribution of
$\hat\mu(x)=\phi(x)^T \hat\beta$ is going to be centered at
$\tilde\mu(x)=\phi(x)^T\tilde\beta$ instead of at
$\mu(x)=E[Y_i \mid X_i=x]$.

2\. The fact that these 'errors' $\tilde \varepsilon_i$ aren't normal
doesn't change much either because an average of independent random
variables with mean zero is always going to be approximately normal.

Now let's try a version of this exercise in which our errors look very
non-normal. As in the last exercise, we'll let $X_i$ be standard normal
and the conditional mean $\mu(x)=E[Y_i \mid X_i=x]$ be $\sin(x)$. But
now our observations are going to be binary: $Y_i \in \{-1,1\}$.

$$ Y_i = \begin{cases} 
\hphantom{-}1 & \text{ with conditional probability }\ \frac{1+\sin(X_i)}{2} \\
-1 & \text{ with conditional probability }\ 1-\frac{1+\sin(X_i)}{2}. \end{cases}
$$

::: exercise
Calculate $E[Y_i \mid X_i=x]$ by hand and show that it's $\sin(x)$.
Either type up your solution in the notebook or turn in a photo of your
work with your submission.
:::

::: solution
To review how to calculate conditional expectations, see FAS Chapter 2.2.3.
$$ 
\begin{aligned}
   E[Y_i \mid X_i=x] 
   &= 1 \cdot P(\{ Y_i = 1\} \mid X_i=x) + (-1) P(\{Y_i = -1\} \mid X_i=x) \\
   &= \frac{1+\sin(x)}{2} - \left\{ 1 - \frac{1+\sin(x)}{2}\right\} \\
   &= \frac{2+2\sin(x)}{2} - 1 = \frac{2 + 2\sin(x)}{2} - \frac{2}{2} = \sin(x).
\end{aligned}
$$
:::

::: exercise
Generate 1000 samples of size 100. For each sample, fit the linear model
$\{m(x)=\beta_0 + \beta_1 x : \beta_0, \beta_1 \in \mathbb{R} \}$; form
a 68% confidence interval using the squared residual
$\hat\varepsilon_i^2 = \{Y_i - \phi(X_i)^T \hat\beta\}^2$ as in the last
section, and report the number of times $\phi(1)^T\tilde\beta$ falls in
the resulting $68\%$ confidence interval.

Try this again at sample sizes $n=5$,$n=20$, and $n=1000$. What happens
with the coverage for $t(\mu)=\mu(1)$? What about for
$t(\tilde\mu)=\tilde\mu(1)$? How does it compare the normal-errors case
from the last exercise?

It's worth thinking about what's going on, but it's ok if you're
stumped. We'll have a homework problem where we'll talk about why what
happens happens.
:::

Here's some code for sampling $Y_i$ this way.

```{r}
Y.positive = rbinom(n, 1, (1+sin(X))/2)
Y = ifelse(Y.positive, 1, -1)
```

::: solution

Basically we see exactly what we saw in the last exercise. Not much changes when the errors aren't
normal. I did, however, prompt you to look at something new: how coverage for $\mu(1)$ and $\tilde\mu(1)$
varies as $n$ does. For $\tilde\mu(1)$, it gets better --- closer to 68% --- as $n$ increases. Basically,
that's what we talked about in our second exercise. It's our Z statistics getting to have a distribution increasingly close to standard normal. Part of that is estimated variance converging to the actual variance of our estimator as before, another part is the estimator itself getting increasingly normal, as predicted by the
central limit theorem, as we start averaging more and more non-normal errors together. 

For $\mu(1)$, it gets worse. What's happening is that our estimator is converging to $\tilde\mu(1)$, which
is not all that close to $\mu(1)$: $\tilde\mu(1) - \mu(1) = 1/\sqrt{e} - \sin(1) \approx = -.23$. In small samples, when the standard deviation of our estimator is large, this difference of $-.23$ doesn't matter much.
In larger samples, when the standard deviation is small, it does. One way of thinking about it is that our
confidence intervals are based on the idea that our Z statistic is normal with mean zero and standard deviation one. In actuality, for $\mu(1)$, they're normal with mean $\approx \{\tilde\mu(1)-\mu(1)\} / \sigma_u$, and as
$\sigma_u$ gets smaller, this becomes further and further away from having mean zero. You can see our two Z
statistic histograms, the purple one for those based on subtracting $\mu(1)$ and the green one for those based on subtracting $\tilde\mu(1)$, moving away from each other as sample size increases. The green one is the one
that's standard normal. At $n=1000$, they don't overlap at all, and essentially as a result our confidence interval *never* contains $\mu(1)$.

```{r}
set.seed(1)

# Describe Distribution of the Data
beta = c(1,2)
phi = function(x) { rbind(1,x) }
mu = sin                                      
mu.tilde = function(x) { x/sqrt(exp(1)) }      
sigma = function(x) { 1/(1+abs(x)) }
n = 1000

Z.stats = array(dim=1000)
Z.stats.mu.tilde = array(dim=1000)
covers  = array(dim=1000)
covers.mu.tilde = array(dim=1000)              
for(rr in 1:1000) {
  # Sample Data
  X = rnorm(n)
  Z = rnorm(n)

  Y.positive = rbinom(n, 1, (1+sin(X))/2)  #   These two lines replaced the lines
  Y = ifelse(Y.positive, 1, -1)            #   sampling Y in our code before

  # Fit model
  model = lm(Y~1+X)
  mu.hat.1 = predict(model, newdata=data.frame(X=1))
  epsilon.hat = Y - predict(model, newdata=data.frame(X=X))                                    
  
  # Calculate V and sigma.hat.u
  Sigma.hat = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { Sigma.hat = Sigma.hat + phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n }
  Sigma.hat.inv = solve(Sigma.hat)
  Sigma.hat.w = array(0, dim=c(2,2))
  for(i in 1:n) { 
    Sigma.hat.w = Sigma.hat.w + epsilon.hat[i]^2 * phi(X[i]) %*% t(phi(X[i]))/n 
  } 
  V = Sigma.hat.inv %*% Sigma.hat.w %*% Sigma.hat.inv
  sigma.hat.u = sqrt( t(phi(1)) %*% V %*% phi(1) / n )
  
  # Calculate Z stat and Interval
  Z = (mu.hat.1 - mu(1) ) / sigma.hat.u # in [-1, 1]
  a = mu.hat.1 - sigma.hat.u
  b = mu.hat.1 + sigma.hat.u
  
  ## Record Results
  Z.stats[rr] = Z
  covers[rr]  = a <= mu(1) & mu(1) <= b

  Z.stats.mu.tilde[rr] = (mu.hat.1 - mu.tilde(1)) /sigma.hat.u  
  covers.mu.tilde[rr] = a <= mu.tilde(1) & mu.tilde(1) <= b
}  

ggplot() + geom_histogram(aes(x=Z.stats), bins=60, fill='purple', alpha=.2) +
           geom_histogram(aes(x=Z.stats.mu.tilde), bins=60, fill='green', alpha=.2) 
mean(covers)
mean(covers.mu.tilde)
```
:::
